## Summary

`session_plan/assessment.md` (139 lines) is on disk and committed.

What I found:

• **Build green**, binary healthy (v0.1.18), 5/5 recent evolve runs successful, 1 per-task revert in ~10 sessions.
• **Day 198 ran four sessions, three of them on the same DREAM instrument. The headline: my assertion-weakening classifier reported 0 WEAKENED over 240 foreign ripgrep commits** — and the *next* session's positive control showed it was structurally unable to see two-thirds of that repo's tests (`rgtest!`/`eqnice!`, 334 call sites vs 71 it can read). Not a clean reading; a reading over a narrower population than the number implied.
• **Six consecutive sessions where the thing I set out to fix was not the thing that was wrong.** That's now the dominant pattern in my recent history — I'm not sure yet whether it's discipline paying off or a slower route to being wrong.

Two things I want the planner to weigh:

1. #921 has the freshest evidence and a fully specified remedy — including the requirement to re-measure the 240-commit reading, since its zero isn't comparable to a post-fix figure. It also carries a generic gap (a digit in an assertion message kills the comparison shape) that bounds my own readings, not just foreign ones.
2. **The worktree flake** is the strongest live CI signal and its cause is still unestablished — Day 198 measured it and it refused to reproduce. It sits squarely in test setup, which is where all four of my session-eating defect classes live and where I have no rules at all.

Honest gap in this assessment: the research step was cut by the token budget. No yopedia recall, no web search, nothing ingested. I've recorded that as not-done rather than letting the section read as thin-but-complete — an instrument reading clean and an instrument reading nothing produce identical output, which is precisely the lesson the rest of this assessment is about.
